WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:05.660
Bill Duke: There is a great book…there are
two books that I would recommend to people.

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:08.450
One is by Steven Pressfield and it’s called
THE WAR OF ART.

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:11.160
It’s one of the greatest books I’ve ever
read.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:16.880
It helped changed my life, THE WAR OF ART.
Patrick Shen: I love…what’s his name?

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:20.029
Steven…do you guys know the book THE WAR
OF ART?

00:00:20.029 --> 00:00:21.109
Is it Steven Pressfield?

00:00:21.109 --> 00:00:22.109
Press field, right?

00:00:22.109 --> 00:00:24.849
Jay Duplass: I do love THE WAR OF ART.

00:00:24.849 --> 00:00:26.240
That’s the one book that I love.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:30.161
UCLA Screenwriting Chair Richard Walter: THE WAR OF
ART…it’s a play on THE ART OF WAR.

00:00:30.161 --> 00:00:35.109
Michael La Pointe: THE WAR OF ART by Steven
Pressfield, it’s a book…it’s not a book

00:00:35.109 --> 00:00:36.109
on screenwriting.

00:00:36.109 --> 00:00:37.399
It’s a book on getting things done.

00:00:37.399 --> 00:00:41.420
Patrick Shen: I love THE WAR OF ART by Steven
Pressfield.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:45.250
That has a great prescription for getting
through writer’s block.

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:50.120
You know you can sort of pull from that to
apply to any sort of challenge that you’re

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:52.539
meeting along the production phase.

00:00:52.539 --> 00:00:58.410
And his advice you know, the bottom line is,
in order to beat writer’s block, you simply

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:00.370
have to continue writing.

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:02.730
And you write each and every day.

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:05.390
And the only way around it is to go through
it.

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:09.329
Michael La Pointe: Do the work as in sit down
and psychically do the work.

00:01:09.329 --> 00:01:14.680
And then the rest of the book kind of talks
about fears and how to get past that but through

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:21.590
the lens of if you just do the work your fears
will go away or at least shrink, which is

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:27.770
true because doing something builds confidence
and confidence builds ambition.

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:34.130
UCLA Screenwriting Chair Richard Walter: And it’s
a pamphlet, it’s a very light piece.

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:37.960
You could read it while you’re waiting for
the light to change.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:38.960
But it’s brilliant.

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:40.510
It is brilliant.

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:45.610
Pressfield, what he calls it is resistance.

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:50.750
There is this resistance sitting there and
you just have to face it and do it.

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:52.780
And you have to face it and you just have
to do it and you have stop trying to like

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:53.780
it.

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:59.969
Jay Duplass: I don’t subscribe to screenwriting
gurus because most of them have not written

00:01:59.969 --> 00:02:01.829
great screenplays.

00:02:01.829 --> 00:02:08.649
I do love THE WAR OF ART, it’s the one book
that I love (Steve Pressfield).

00:02:08.649 --> 00:02:12.620
And I don’t think it’s a coincidence.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:14.610
He’s a good screenwriter.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:20.010
He’s one of the few who has written great
things.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:25.010
But to me it’s not about form and it’s
not about teaching you how to do it.

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:28.720
It’s more about inspiring you and getting
you unstuck.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:31.650
And I think that book has been super helpful
to me.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:33.930
UCLA Screenwriting Chair Richard Walter: He’s gone
over the notes.

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:39.340
He’s thought everything through and he’s
about to start the actual rewrite and he's

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:44.519
just miserable contemplating actually getting
into the actual re-write.

00:02:44.519 --> 00:02:48.810
And I said to him “I’m glad to hear it.”

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:50.849
Why am I glad to hear that he is miserable?

00:02:50.849 --> 00:02:55.270
Because he’s telling me about the experiences
a real writer would have.

00:02:55.270 --> 00:03:05.421
Only an amateur (Pressfield would out it this
way) would say that she or he can’t wait

00:03:05.421 --> 00:03:10.519
to get into the rewrite, is delighted contemplating
getting up in the morning and working on that

00:03:10.519 --> 00:03:11.580
script.

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.490
The contrary is the case, dreading it.

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:16.330
Michael La Pointe: It’s definitely…if
you are in any kind of creative field, you’re

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:20.790
stuck, even if you’re not stuck and you
are just starting out, it’s would probably

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:26.920
be more beneficial to you than the standard
screenwriting book that is going to tell you

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:32.610
how to do screenwriting but not really tell
you how to be creative which is like a big

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:33.610
thing.

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:45.710
There is all these books on how to manually
do screenwriting, how to break down a movie

00:03:45.710 --> 00:03:51.600
into a 3-act structure, how do you write a
3-act structure movie, how do you do The Hero’s

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:52.600
Journey?

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:57.620
There is very little on how you psychically
do it.

00:03:57.620 --> 00:04:03.260
There is very little (this is obviously a
very personal opinion)…the two most important

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:08.280
parts of filmmaking are creativity and originality.

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:10.099
And there are very little books on that.

00:04:10.099 --> 00:04:13.630
You know there are kind of books in the art
vein of how do you be original?

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:15.620
But there is very little on filmmaking and
all that.

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:18.270
And that is what we admire about filmmaking.

00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:23.420
There are great movies that are 3-act structure
Hollywood movies that nobody really likes

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:28.810
and there are things…like BEAST OF THE SOUTHERN
WILD that is something which is very original.

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:32.720
That is something that people respond to.

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:38.630
So it’s kind of odd that there is so many
books on this one subject and so little on

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:42.010
something that matters so much (to me…it’s
a personal opinion) about filmmaking.

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:44.630
UCLA Screenwriting Chair Richard Walter: The real writers
hate to write.

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:47.280
All writers that are serious writers, they
don’t like to write.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:54.610
We love having written, but actually writing
is always painful “Is it good enough?

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:59.820
I thought yesterday what I did was…now I’m
re-reading it and it’s not as good as I’m

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:00.820
remembering it.

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:02.130
And it’s very frustrating.

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:03.480
Do I dare show this to anybody?

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:08.980
How can I expect people to (in exchange for
this) give me money so that I can put orthodontia

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:11.300
on my children’s teeth and so forth.

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:18.110
This guy said to me it was such a liberation
to read that…for him to read that all writers

00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:24.320
hate to write and to discover that he’s
not the only that doesn’t leap from his

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:30.880
bed in the morning…you know…with bells
on eager beyond description to get into his

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:32.200
writing.

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:33.520
No.

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:39.410
We all want to put it off, delay it, defer
it, it’s that way for everybody.

